DONE
[x] #3 Editor IntelliSense (dependency types) - implemented + server-side verified
    New files:
      - src/lib/type-libs.ts        enumerates type entry points + tsconfig -> monaco enums
      - src/app/api/types/route.ts  GET -> { compilerOptions, libs, stats } (cached)
    Changed:
      - src/components/ide/monaco-pane.tsx: loadTypeLibs() called from onMount;
        setCompilerOptions + addExtraLib per .d.ts + setEagerModelSync(true)
    Verified: tsc 0, eslint 0, endpoint 200
      target 'ES2018' -> 5, module 'esnext' -> 99, jsx 'react-jsx' -> 4,
      moduleResolution 'bundler' -> 2 (NodeJs fallback), lib + paths carried over
    Payload: 20 packages, 1957 files, 12.76 MB  (see follow-ups)

    Two real bugs found and fixed while verifying (both would have silently broken it):
      1. I had guessed Monaco's enum numbers. They come from monaco-editor's OWN bundled
         TypeScript (monaco.d.ts), not the workspace `typescript` package - and the two
         disagree: the workspace TS uses STRING enums ("es2018"), Monaco uses NUMERIC
         (ES2018 = 5). Corrected against monaco.d.ts.
      2. tsconfig writes `"jsx": "react-jsx"` but the lookup keys were `reactjsx`
         (no hyphen), so jsx was never set - React/JSX IntelliSense, the whole point,
         was dead. Keys are now hyphenated (plus the bare form).

NOT VERIFIED
[ ] The in-editor experience. I verified the data layer end to end but have no browser
    access, so nobody has actually seen completions appear. Needs a visual check by hand.

FOLLOW-UPS for #3
[ ] Payload is 12.76 MB. lucide-react alone is 5.84 MB, and 3.84 MB of that is
    lucide-react.{prefixed,suffixed}.d.ts - alternate entry points nobody imports.
    Real fix: load types on demand per import (scan the buffer, request only the
    specifiers it uses). Typical file would drop to ~100 KB.
[ ] Project-local imports still do not resolve, so `@/lib/...` keeps its squiggles.
    paths is set but baseUrl is absent, and the open file's Monaco model has no `path`,
    so it has an anonymous in-memory URI. Fix: pass `path={`file:///${tab.path}`}` to
    <Editor> and feed src/**/*.ts(x) in as extra libs too.
[ ] The bundle is cached in globalThis for the server process, so installing a new
    dependency needs a server restart before its types show up.
[ ] Same optional-path problem affects the earlier env-leak item.

=== STATUS UPDATE #3 - 2026-09-17 (Dovee agent) ===

DONE (IntelliSense follow-ups)
[x] Project-local imports now resolve - the `@/lib/...` squiggles should be gone
    - monaco-pane.tsx: added `path={`file:///${tab.path}`}` so the model URI matches
      the extra-lib URIs.
    - type-libs.ts: baseUrl is now "file:///" (matches the worker's virtual FS root,
      so tsconfig `paths: {"@/*": ["./src/*"]}` actually resolves).
    - type-libs.ts: workspace sources (src/** plus root *.ts) are fed in as extra libs.
    58 src files + 2 root files + 1957 node_modules = 2017 files, 13 MB.

    Verified before relying on it (these were real risks, not guesses):
      - monaco tsWorker `_getScriptText` checks the live MODEL before `_extraLibs`,
        so the open file is always type-checked from the live buffer - an extra lib
        can never shadow your edits.
      - @monaco-editor/react syncs `value` into a reused model via executeEdits, so
        adding `path` does not leave stale content behind (agent edits still land).
      - Bonus found while checking: `path` enables saveViewState/restoreViewState,
        so cursor + scroll position now survive tab switches.

Bugs I introduced and caught in the same session (worth remembering)
[!] A single SKIP_DIRS set was applied to BOTH the project walk and the node_modules
    walk. It contained "dist", which is exactly where packages keep their .d.ts
    (next/dist/**, lucide-react/dist/**). Result: dependency types silently dropped
    from 1957 files / 13.4 MB down to ~398 / 4.6 MB. Now two sets:
    SKIP_PROJECT_DIRS (with dist/.next/build/...) and SKIP_PACKAGE_DIRS (node_modules
    only). Lesson: the "payload shrank" signal was the only thing that revealed it -
    worth keeping an eye on stats after any change to this loader.

Housekeeping
- tsconfig.json / next-env.d.ts / tsconfig.tsbuildinfo are all rewritten or touched by
  `next dev` / `next build`. Commit them once so they stop showing as modified.